=begin
      author: Fizzlesticks
        name: playsongs
        game: gemstone
        tags: bard, tambourine, performance

  Background script that keeps Fizzlesticks playing an instrument.

  Gets the instrument from the backpack, plays it, then watches for the
  game's "performance ended" messages. When one shows up, it pauses briefly
  and plays again.

  Each song cycle (from "you lift your instrument and begin..." to one of
  the "song ended" messages) is timed, and any PLAY skill rank-up messages
  noticed along the way are counted. Per gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning
  that message fires once per rank out of 20 total, each taking exponentially
  longer than the last (rank 5 ~3h48m, rank 10 ~18h12m, rank 15 ~50h, rank
  20/Adept ~115h18m) -- so a low count over a few hours is expected, not a
  sign of missed messages. Every 5 completed songs it also runs PLAY SKILL
  directly and saves the actual "Your current skill level in X instruments
  is Y" results as a second confirmation, shown in playsongs stats. Stats
  are tracked per instrument in a small YAML file at
  data/<game>/<character>/playsongs_stats.yaml.

  A song that ends in under 285 seconds (MIN_SONG_SECONDS minus a
  SONG_LENGTH_TOLERANCE grace period) is treated as aborted -- e.g.
  interrupted before it could finish naturally -- and isn't counted toward
  songs_played/total_seconds, though any rank-ups noticed during it still
  are. The grace period exists because a genuine completion can land a
  few seconds under the ~300s target.

  Auto-pauses (no command needed) for as long as ;eloot sell is running
  (not eloot alone), and resumes once it finishes -- see EXCLUSIVE_SCRIPTS
  to add others.

  Usage:  ;playsongs                (defaults to tambourine, played soft)
          ;playsongs lute           (play a different instrument instead)
          ;playsongs tambourine fast   (style: soft (default), fast, or slow)

  Set a persistent default instrument (used whenever no instrument is
  given on the command line) with:
          ;vars set instrument=lute
  A command-line instrument always overrides that setting -- ";playsongs
  tambourine" plays tambourine for that run even if instrument is set to
  lute in ;vars.

  Commands -- "stats" and "help" work either way (;playsongs stats or just
  typing playsongs stats), including before the script has ever been
  started. "pause"/"resume" need it already running, so use the no-
  semicolon form for those while it's going in the background:
          playsongs stats           - print stats for every instrument
                                       tracked so far
          playsongs pause           - stop starting new songs after the
                                       current one finishes
          playsongs resume          - resume after a pause
          playsongs help            - show this help

  Stop:   ;kill playsongs
=end

require 'yaml'
require 'fileutils'

module Playsongs

  COMMANDS = %w[stats help pause resume].freeze

  # @param word [String, nil]
  # @return [String, nil] the COMMANDS entry word is an unambiguous
  #   (3+ char) prefix of, e.g. "res"/"resum" -> "resume", or nil
  def self.match_command(word)
    return nil unless word

    w = word.downcase
    COMMANDS.find { |c| w.length >= 3 && c.start_with?(w) }
  end

  # vars[1] doubles as both the instrument name (";playsongs lute") and a
  # command (";playsongs help") -- resolve which one it is before deciding
  # INSTRUMENT, so "help"/"stats"/etc. never end up as a literal instrument
  # name (e.g. when the script isn't running yet, so the UpstreamHook
  # trigger below -- which only fires for the no-semicolon typed form --
  # never gets a chance to handle it).
  RAW_FIRST_ARG = Script.current.vars[1]
  EARLY_COMMAND = match_command(RAW_FIRST_ARG)

  # Persistent default instrument, set with: ;vars set instrument=lute
  # A command-line arg (";playsongs lute") always wins over this when given;
  # this is only the fallback used when none is passed. Hardcoded
  # "tambourine" is the last resort if neither is set.
  DEFAULT_INSTRUMENT = (UserVars.instrument || "tambourine").to_s.downcase

  INSTRUMENT = (EARLY_COMMAND ? DEFAULT_INSTRUMENT : (RAW_FIRST_ARG || DEFAULT_INSTRUMENT)).downcase

  STYLES = %w[soft fast slow]
  requested_style = (Script.current.vars[2] || "soft").downcase
  unless STYLES.include?(requested_style)
    echo "Unknown style '#{requested_style}', defaulting to soft. Valid styles: #{STYLES.join(', ')}."
    requested_style = "soft"
  end
  STYLE = requested_style

  START_REGEX = /You lift your \S+ and begin to tap out a beat\./

  # Per https://gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning, this message fires once
  # per rank (not per skill tick), and there are only 20 ranks total, with
  # each one taking exponentially longer than the last (rank 5 ~3h48m,
  # rank 10 ~18h12m, rank 15 ~50h, rank 20/Adept ~115h18m). So a low count
  # over a few hours is expected, not a sign this is missing messages --
  # it just won't fire often. Kept in sync with the periodic PLAY SKILL
  # check (SKILL_LEVEL_REGEX/maybe_check_skill) as a second confirmation of
  # the same authoritative tier, shown in playsongs stats.
  RANKUP_REGEX = /You feel a sense of accomplishment as you continue practicing your \S+!/

  SKILL_LEVEL_REGEX = /Your current skill level in (.+?) instruments is (\w+)\.?/i

  # Some instruments (e.g. piccolo) need both hands, so having the right
  # hand free for a weapon per ensure_in_left_hand isn't always possible --
  # this catches the game telling us so and reacts by stowing the right
  # hand and retrying, rather than needing a hardcoded list of which
  # instruments are two-handed.
  TWO_HANDED_REGEX = /requires two hands to play, try emptying your right hand\./

  # "your \S+" instead of the instrument name so this still matches regardless
  # of what's being played; "you"/"You" scopes it to your own performance so
  # it won't trigger off other bards finishing theirs.
  STOP_REGEX = Regexp.union(
    /As the last whispered note fades from the stilled air, you slowly smile and put your hand over your \S+, bowing with a flourish\./,
    /The music moves slower and slower until finally, with a last hovering beat, it fades away\. You look up from your \S+ and smile\./,
    /The air suddenly stills as you halt your whirling rhythm as suddenly as you began, silencing your \S+\./,
    /You complete the last beat with a flourish, lowering your \S+\./,
    /With quiet grace, you allow your \S+'s melody to drop off, ever softer, until there is nothing but the intense silence left in its wake\./
  )

  RESTART_PAUSE_SECONDS = 3
  PAUSE_POLL_SECONDS = 1
  SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL = 5   # run PLAY SKILL every this many completed songs
  MIN_SONG_SECONDS = 300     # ~5 min per gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning -- a real song's target length
  SONG_LENGTH_TOLERANCE = 15 # grace period below MIN_SONG_SECONDS -- timing/detection can land a few
                              # seconds early on a genuine completion (e.g. 299s), which shouldn't get
                              # treated the same as an actually-interrupted song

  # [rank, cumulative seconds] checkpoints from
  # https://gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning -- (0, 0) added as a
  # starting point so there's a full curve to interpolate/extrapolate
  # against, not just the four wiki data points.
  MILESTONES = [[0, 0], [5, 13_680], [10, 65_520], [15, 180_000], [20, 415_080]].freeze

  TIER_RANGES = { 'novice' => 1..5, 'apprentice' => 6..13, 'journeyman' => 14..19, 'adept' => 20..20 }.freeze

  # Very rough instrument -> PLAY SKILL category guesses, just enough to
  # cross-reference the estimate below against the real tier from
  # maybe_check_skill. Add more as needed.
  CATEGORY_HINTS = {
    'percussion' => %w[tambourine drum bongo cymbal],
    'woodwind' => %w[flute whistle fife piccolo],
    'stringed' => %w[lute harp mandolin fiddle guitar lyre],
    'brass' => %w[horn trumpet trombone]
  }.freeze

  # @return [Float] estimated rank (0-20) for total seconds played, via
  #   linear interpolation between MILESTONES, clamped at 20
  def self.estimated_rank(seconds)
    return 20.0 if seconds >= MILESTONES.last[1]

    MILESTONES.each_cons(2) do |(r1, s1), (r2, s2)|
      next unless seconds.between?(s1, s2)

      return r1 + (seconds - s1).to_f / (s2 - s1) * (r2 - r1)
    end
    0.0
  end

  # @return [Integer] estimated cumulative seconds needed to reach a rank
  def self.time_for_rank(rank)
    return MILESTONES.last[1] if rank >= MILESTONES.last[0]

    MILESTONES.each_cons(2) do |(r1, s1), (r2, s2)|
      next unless rank.between?(r1, r2)

      return (s1 + (rank - r1).to_f / (r2 - r1) * (s2 - s1)).round
    end
    0
  end

  # @return [String, nil] tier name ("novice", "apprentice", ...) for an
  #   estimated (possibly fractional) rank
  def self.tier_for_rank(rank)
    TIER_RANGES.find { |_tier, range| range.cover?(rank.round) }&.first
  end

  # @param instrument_name [String]
  # @return [String, nil] best-guess PLAY SKILL category name for it
  def self.category_for(instrument_name)
    CATEGORY_HINTS.find { |_category, names| names.any? { |n| instrument_name.include?(n) } }&.first
  end

  # Scripts that need your hands/attention -- playsongs auto-pauses (same as
  # a manual "playsongs pause") for as long as a running script matches one
  # of these, and auto-resumes once it finishes, no command needed either
  # way. arg_pattern nil means any invocation of that script name matches;
  # otherwise at least one of the script's args must match the pattern.
  EXCLUSIVE_SCRIPTS = [
    { name: 'eloot', arg_pattern: /sell/i } # only "eloot sell", not eloot alone
  ]

  STATS_FILE = File.join(DATA_DIR, XMLData.game, Char.name, 'playsongs_stats.yaml')

  @paused = false
  @pause_mutex = Mutex.new
  @song_count = 0
  @blocking_script = nil
  @was_paused = false

  def self.pause!
    @pause_mutex.synchronize { @paused = true }
  end

  def self.resume!
    @pause_mutex.synchronize { @paused = false }
  end

  def self.paused?
    @pause_mutex.synchronize { @paused }
  end

  # @return [String, nil] "name arg1 arg2..." for the first running script
  #   matching an EXCLUSIVE_SCRIPTS entry, or nil if none match
  def self.blocking_script
    running = Script.list
    EXCLUSIVE_SCRIPTS.each do |spec|
      match = running.find do |s|
        s.name == spec[:name] &&
          (spec[:arg_pattern].nil? || s.vars.any? { |v| v.to_s =~ spec[:arg_pattern] })
      end
      # vars[0] is the whole args string joined back together (e.g. "sell"),
      # and vars[1..] are the individual tokens -- using the full vars array
      # here double-counts a single-word arg ("eloot sell sell").
      return [match.name, *match.vars[1..]].join(' ') if match
    end
    nil
  end

  def self.load_stats
    return {} unless File.exist?(STATS_FILE)

    YAML.load_file(STATS_FILE) || {}
  end

  def self.save_stats(stats)
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(STATS_FILE))
    File.write(STATS_FILE, stats.to_yaml)
  end

  def self.record_song(duration_seconds, rank_ups)
    stats = load_stats
    entry = stats[INSTRUMENT] ||= { 'songs_played' => 0, 'rank_ups' => 0, 'total_seconds' => 0 }
    entry['songs_played'] += 1
    entry['rank_ups'] += rank_ups
    entry['total_seconds'] += duration_seconds.round
    entry['last_played'] = Time.now.to_s
    save_stats(stats)
    entry
  end

  # Rank-ups are a real skill event even if the song itself gets aborted
  # early, so they're still worth keeping -- just without touching
  # songs_played/total_seconds for a song that didn't really happen.
  def self.record_rank_ups_only(rank_ups)
    return if rank_ups.zero?

    stats = load_stats
    entry = stats[INSTRUMENT] ||= { 'songs_played' => 0, 'rank_ups' => 0, 'total_seconds' => 0 }
    entry['rank_ups'] += rank_ups
    save_stats(stats)
    entry
  end

  # @param total_seconds [Integer]
  # @return [String] "1h 23m 45s" (omitting leading zero units, but always
  #   showing seconds)
  def self.format_duration(total_seconds)
    hours, remainder = total_seconds.divmod(3600)
    minutes, seconds = remainder.divmod(60)

    parts = []
    parts << "#{hours}h" if hours > 0
    parts << "#{minutes}m" if hours > 0 || minutes > 0
    parts << "#{seconds}s"
    parts.join(' ')
  end

  # Prints every instrument's tracked stats to the local window.
  def self.show_stats
    stats = load_stats

    respond ''
    respond '--- Playsongs Stats ---'
    if stats.empty?
      respond 'No stats recorded yet.'
    else
      stats.each do |instrument, entry|
        songs = entry['songs_played'].to_i
        seconds = entry['total_seconds'].to_i
        avg = songs > 0 ? (seconds / songs.to_f).round(1) : 0
        respond "#{instrument}: #{songs} songs, #{entry['rank_ups'].to_i} rank-ups, #{format_duration(seconds)} played (avg #{avg}s/song), last played #{entry['last_played'] || 'never'}"

        levels = entry['skill_levels']
        if levels && !levels.empty?
          levels_str = levels.map { |category, level| "#{category}: #{level}" }.join(', ')
          respond "  PLAY SKILL (as of #{entry['skill_checked_at']}): #{levels_str}"
        end

        # Just for fun -- https://gswiki.play.net/Instrument_learning-based
        # estimate of rank from tracked hours alone, cross-checked against
        # the real tier from the PLAY SKILL check above. seconds here only
        # covers time this script tracked, so it'll almost always look like
        # you're "ahead of schedule" -- that gap is prior manual practice
        # and/or aborted songs this script never counted, not extra-fast
        # learning.
        est_rank = estimated_rank(seconds)
        respond "  Estimate from tracked time alone: rank ~#{est_rank.round(1)} (#{tier_for_rank(est_rank)})"

        category = category_for(instrument)
        actual_tier = levels && category ? levels[category] : nil
        if actual_tier && TIER_RANGES[actual_tier.downcase]
          expected_seconds = time_for_rank(TIER_RANGES[actual_tier.downcase].first)
          diff = expected_seconds - seconds
          if diff > 0
            respond "  That's #{format_duration(diff)} less than the estimate for reaching #{actual_tier} (~#{format_duration(expected_seconds)}) -- there was likely some practice time and aborted songs not tracked here."
          else
            respond "  Right on pace (or ahead) of the estimate for reaching #{actual_tier}."
          end
        end
      end
    end
    respond ''
  end

  # Prints available commands to the local window.
  def self.show_help
    respond ''
    respond '--- Playsongs Help ---'
    respond "Keeps playing an instrument (#{STYLE} style) in a loop, timing each song and counting rank-ups."
    respond "Also runs PLAY SKILL every #{SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL} completed songs and saves the real skill level as a second confirmation of the rank-up count."
    respond "Auto-pauses while eloot sell is running (not eloot alone), no command needed."
    respond ''
    respond 'Commands (typed directly, no leading semicolon):'
    respond '  playsongs stats    - print stats for every instrument tracked so far'
    respond '  playsongs pause    - stop starting new songs after the current one finishes'
    respond '  playsongs resume   - resume after a pause'
    respond '  playsongs help     - show this help'
    respond ''
    respond "Default instrument: set with ;vars set instrument=<name> (currently: #{DEFAULT_INSTRUMENT}). A command-line instrument always overrides it."
    respond ''
    respond 'Stop the script entirely with: ;kill playsongs'
    respond ''
  end

  def self.save_skill_levels(levels)
    return if levels.empty?

    stats = load_stats
    entry = stats[INSTRUMENT] ||= { 'songs_played' => 0, 'rank_ups' => 0, 'total_seconds' => 0 }
    entry['skill_levels'] = levels
    entry['skill_checked_at'] = Time.now.to_s
    save_stats(stats)
  end

  # Every SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL completed songs, runs PLAY SKILL and captures
  # its response (e.g. "Your current skill level in percussion instruments
  # is Apprentice.") -- this is the game's own authoritative skill report,
  # unlike the rough RANKUP_REGEX counter, and gets saved for playsongs
  # stats to display.
  def self.maybe_check_skill(song_count)
    return unless (song_count % SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL).zero?

    echo "Periodic PLAY SKILL check (every #{SKILL_CHECK_INTERVAL} songs) --"
    lines = Lich::Util.issue_command("play skill", SKILL_LEVEL_REGEX, /<prompt/, include_end: false, silent: true)

    levels = {}
    Array(lines).each { |line| levels[$1.downcase] = $2 if line =~ SKILL_LEVEL_REGEX }
    save_skill_levels(levels)
  end

  # No "from my backpack" -- the game sometimes auto-stows the instrument
  # into whatever container has room (e.g. when both hands are needed for
  # something else), not necessarily the backpack, so this needs to find it
  # wherever it landed.
  def self.get_instrument
    fput "get my #{INSTRUMENT}"
  end

  # INSTRUMENT belongs in the left hand so the right hand stays free for a
  # weapon. Fixes wherever it currently is: GETs it if it's not in hand at
  # all, or swaps hands if it landed in the right hand (other scripts love
  # to put things away, not always into the same hand).
  # @return [Boolean] true if it was already correctly in the left hand
  def self.ensure_in_left_hand
    fput "glance"

    if checkleft(INSTRUMENT)
      true
    elsif checkright(INSTRUMENT)
      echo "#{INSTRUMENT} is in my right hand -- swapping so my right hand is free for a weapon."
      fput "swap"
      false
    else
      get_instrument
      false
    end
  end

  # Sends the play command, watches the response for the song actually
  # starting and any rank-up messages along the way, then waits for one of
  # the "song ended" messages. Records the completed cycle to STATS_FILE.
  def self.play_and_track
    started_at = Time.now
    rank_ups = 0

    fput "play my #{INSTRUMENT} #{STYLE}"

    loop do
      line = get
      if line =~ TWO_HANDED_REGEX
        echo "#{INSTRUMENT} needs both hands -- stowing right hand and retrying."
        fput "stow right"
        started_at = Time.now
        fput "play my #{INSTRUMENT} #{STYLE}"
        next
      end
      started_at = Time.now if line =~ START_REGEX
      if line =~ RANKUP_REGEX
        rank_ups += 1
        Lich::Messaging.msg("bold", "[Playsongs] Rank up! #{line.strip}")
      end
      break if line =~ STOP_REGEX
    end

    # Round once and use that consistently below -- comparing the raw float
    # against MIN_SONG_SECONDS while displaying the rounded value could
    # print e.g. "300s ... under 300s" for a song that was really 299.6s.
    duration = (Time.now - started_at).round

    if duration < MIN_SONG_SECONDS - SONG_LENGTH_TOLERANCE
      record_rank_ups_only(rank_ups)
      echo "Song aborted after #{duration}s (under #{MIN_SONG_SECONDS}s) -- not counted toward song stats."
    else
      entry = record_song(duration, rank_ups)
      summary = "[Playsongs] Song complete in #{duration}s (rank-ups: #{rank_ups}). #{INSTRUMENT} totals: #{entry['songs_played']} songs, #{entry['rank_ups']} rank-ups, #{format_duration(entry['total_seconds'])} played."
      # echo/respond force <output class="mono"/> and HTML-escape <, > --
      # which mangles pushBold/popBold into inert text. Lich::Messaging.msg
      # goes through _respond instead (no mono-wrap/escaping), so the color
      # tags actually survive to the client.
      Lich::Messaging.msg("bold", summary)
    end
  end

  # ";playsongs help"/";playsongs stats" work standalone (before the script
  # is even running) since vars[1] resolved to a command instead of an
  # instrument up above. "pause"/"resume" don't make sense without a
  # running instance to control, so just say so instead of starting one.
  if EARLY_COMMAND
    case EARLY_COMMAND
    when 'stats'
      show_stats
    when 'help'
      show_help
    when 'pause', 'resume'
      echo "Not running yet -- start with ;playsongs [instrument] first, then use 'playsongs #{EARLY_COMMAND}' (no semicolon) once it's going."
    end
    exit
  end

  echo "Playsongs started -- instrument: #{INSTRUMENT}, style: #{STYLE}"

  # Lets typing "playsongs <command>" (no leading semicolon) control the
  # running script without being sent upstream as a game command.
  # Registered as a plain UpstreamHook (not tied to this script's own loop)
  # so it still works while playsongs is already running in the background
  # -- ";playsongs stats" would just hit Lich's "already running" guard
  # instead.
  UpstreamHook.remove('playsongs-trigger') if UpstreamHook.list.include?('playsongs-trigger')
  UpstreamHook.add('playsongs-trigger', proc { |client_string|
    match = client_string.strip.match(/^(?:<c>)?playsongs\s+(\S+)$/i)
    if match
      word = match[1].downcase
      command = Playsongs.match_command(word)
      case command
      when 'stats'
        Playsongs.show_stats
      when 'help'
        Playsongs.show_help
      when 'pause'
        Playsongs.pause!
        respond '[Playsongs] Pausing after the current song finishes. Resume with: playsongs resume'
      when 'resume'
        # Just flips the flag -- fput (and anything that calls it, like
        # ensure_in_left_hand) requires Script.current, which is nil from
        # this UpstreamHook callback since it runs on Lich's input thread,
        # not the script's own thread. The main loop (running on the
        # script's actual thread) picks up the cleared flag within
        # PAUSE_POLL_SECONDS, prints its own "playing again in Ns" countdown,
        # and does the hand check/fix and play from there instead.
        Playsongs.resume!
      else
        respond "[Playsongs] Unknown command '#{word}'. Try: playsongs help"
      end
      nil
    else
      client_string
    end
  })

  if ensure_in_left_hand
    echo "Already holding #{INSTRUMENT} in my left hand, skipping get."
  end

  loop do
    blocker = blocking_script
    if blocker
      if @blocking_script != blocker
        echo "Pausing while #{blocker} is running."
        @blocking_script = blocker
      end
      sleep PAUSE_POLL_SECONDS
      next
    elsif @blocking_script
      echo "#{@blocking_script} finished -- resuming."
      @blocking_script = nil
    end

    if paused?
      @was_paused = true
      sleep PAUSE_POLL_SECONDS
      next
    elsif @was_paused
      @was_paused = false
      echo "Resumed -- playing #{INSTRUMENT} again in #{RESTART_PAUSE_SECONDS} seconds..."
      sleep RESTART_PAUSE_SECONDS
    end

    # The game sometimes auto-stows the instrument between songs (e.g. hands
    # needed for something else), which also fires the same "song ended"
    # messages this script watches for -- so always confirm it's still
    # correctly in the left hand before trying to play again instead of
    # assuming the last song ended because it actually finished.
    ensure_in_left_hand
    play_and_track
    @song_count += 1
    maybe_check_skill(@song_count)
    sleep RESTART_PAUSE_SECONDS
  end

end #End of module
